=== p2-windows · W7 · page:settings/Safety (WinUI 3 / Fluent) ===
Parity unit : page:settings/Safety   kind=page   web route=/settings/safety
Web source  : web/src/features/settings/pages/SafetyPage.tsx
Output      : apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/SafetyPage/ (SafetyPage.Model.cs / .Source.cs / .ViewModel.cs / .cs)
ADR refs    : ADR-002, ADR-004, ADR-005, ADR-006, ADR-011, ADR-013, ADR-014, ADR-015
Runner      : .NET 10.0.300 SDK present (dotnet --version 10.0.300) — NOT blocked.

=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), <Name>.Source.cs (the generated-client source), <Name>.ViewModel.cs
  (INotifyPropertyChanged + IDisposable state holder) and <Name>.cs (the Fluent view), exactly as the sibling W7
  SettingsPage / QuietHoursPage do. The generated prompt's idealized "SafetyPage.xaml + SafetyPageViewModel.cs under
  TeslaSync/Features/Settings" is mapped onto these real conventions (namespace TeslaSync.App.FeatureViews.Settings).
- Naming: the web page is SafetyPage at /settings/safety. RouteTable.cs already maps
  Page("SafetySettingsPage","settings/safety",SettingsAccountIntegrations) — route NAME "SafetySettingsPage" — which
  was the only Settings/safety route NOT yet wired to a page factory (it fell back to RoutePendingView). A DIFFERENT,
  already-built VehicleSystems page (class FeatureViews.VehicleSystems.SafetySettingsPage, route name "SafetySettings",
  path safety-settings, web vehicle-systems/pages/SafetySettingsPage.tsx) keeps the SafetySettingsPage class name, so
  the new class is named SafetyPage (mirroring the web filename) to avoid the clash, and is registered under the
  existing route name "SafetySettingsPage" (SafetyPageRegistration.RouteName) in ShellWindow.xaml.cs.
- Scope (manifest is the source of truth): apps/parity/parity-manifest.json scopes page:settings/Safety to exactly 8
  items — 1 panel (GlassPanel1, note "component: GlassPanel"), 1 state (success) and 6 strings. The manifest declares
  NO API data sources ("renders from navigation params / local state"): the web page reads the seven safety values from
  the already-resolved global useSettings() hook and renders its deterministic, AI-OFF-safe listing unconditionally —
  it never gates a per-page loading/empty/error branch. The native port reproduces exactly that: a single success state.
- Full-listing fidelity (ADR-002/006, no parity shortcuts): the web GlassPanel1 listing is reproduced in full — the
  section header, ALL seven SAFETY_ROWS (quiet-hours enabled / window start / window end / alert digest mode /
  critical-alert tab flash / unread tab badge / API kill-switch), each with its title, an Info TsBadge showing the
  current value formatted exactly as the web renderValue (On/Off, the HH:MM window, the digest enum, Active/Suspended,
  em-dash for blank), its plain-English description and a "Docs" link, plus the read-only change hint.
- Navigation entry (ADR / W4 seam): route name "SafetySettingsPage" (RouteTable path settings/safety) already exists.
  Registration (ShellWindow.xaml.cs) is a static factory new FeatureViews.Settings.SafetyPage() keyed on
  SafetyPageRegistration.RouteName, mirroring the sibling Settings/QuietHours registrations that mount over their inert
  default source (the host-injection precedent the W7 pages set).
- Data binding (ADR-004): the manifest data source is useSettings (GET /settings). The view-model reads through an
  injected ISafetySettingsSource; the default EmptySafetySettingsSource resolves the web DEFAULT_SETTINGS slice (so the
  listing always renders the deterministic baseline), and the generated-client-backed SafetySettingsClientSource
  (SafetyPage.Source.cs) binds the generated OpenAPI contract client operation get_api_v1_settings. No HTTP touches the
  view; the read JSON round-trips through the tolerant SafetySettingsSnapshot.FromJson (accepts the bare object + the
  platform {data:…} envelope and merges each web default for any absent/mistyped field).
- Single data state (manifest: success): SafetyState has the one member the manifest declares — Success. The view-model
  starts in Success with the web-default listing and re-projects with the install's real values once the read resolves;
  a failed read folds back to the defaults (SafetyPageViewModel.LoadAsync catch -> SafetySettingsSnapshot.Default), so
  the listing is never blank — the web pessimistic, defaults-merged contract.
- Panel GlassPanel1: SafetyPage.cs#BuildListingPanel mounts one TsGlassPanel (the W3 Fluent glass/Mica card, ADR-005)
  carrying a SectionTitle + Text header, the seven-row StackPanel (SafetyRowControls, each a Text title + Info TsBadge +
  Caption description + HyperlinkButton docs) and a Caption change-hint; AutomationId "safety-settings-listing" mirrors
  the web data-testid. Per-row AutomationId "safety-settings-row-{key}" mirrors the web li data-testid.
- AI explainer (ADR-015 §I3 hidden UI): the web page can layer an opt-in <AISafetySettingExplainer> ABOVE the listing;
  in off-mode (ai_mode='off') it is absent from the DOM entirely. The native baseline omits it (the deterministic
  listing is the canonical surface; the AI explainer is its own opt-in unit), exactly as the off-mode static-help
  invariant requires — and as the sibling QuietHoursPage gates its AI advisor off.
- Live freshness / polling (ADR-013): N/A — settings is a one-shot REST read with no SSE/live signal source and no
  refetch interval on the web page; no >2-min staleness pill applies.
- SI units (ADR / P1-S5): N/A — this surface bears no SI quantity values; the row values are booleans, an enum string
  and HH:MM clock times (not unit-bearing measurements), so no SI converter applies. No non-SI value is stored/computed.
- i18n (ADR-014): all 6 page/listing keys + the 14 row keys are present verbatim (web key names) in
  Strings/{en,ar,he}/Resources.resw as translation.* with the web English values; the surface resolves them through the
  ILocalizer facade with the web key names + English defaults (PassthroughLocalizer in tests records every key). No
  hardcoded visible chrome literal — the row VALUE tokens (On/Off/Active/Suspended/em-dash) reproduce the web's
  non-i18n renderValue projection verbatim (value tokens, not chrome).
- a11y (ADR-015): the PageContainer carries the page heading-level-1 landmark so the UserControl wrapper is
  AccessibilityView.Raw and exposes a Group automation peer; each row value badge carries AutomationProperties.Name
  "{title}: {value}", each docs link carries Name "{Docs}: {title}" + a ToolTip of the URL; design tokens drive
  light/dark; the HyperlinkButton + badges are keyboard- and pointer-reachable.

